# A real macOS .app: a Cocoa window, an icon, generated plists, and the
# resource files the program reads at run time. Everything except the Bundle
# key is an ordinary build file. Bundling is a packaging step on top of a
# normal executable, not a different kind of project.

Assert.Platform macos

Assembly        Sundial
SourceDirectory src

Version(define) 1.2.0
TitleName       Sundial
Description     Tells the time the old way
CompanyName     Artisan Softworks
Copyright       Copyright (C) 2026 Artisan Softworks

Icon Sundial

Apple.Frameworks Cocoa

# This is the flag that actually triggers the macOS app bundle build just after linking the executable
Apple.Bundle

# Anything the generated Info.plist does not already say goes in as
# Info.plist.<key>. Values are written as <string> unless they are whole
# numbers (<integer>) or written as "(a b c)", which becomes an <array>.
# NSHumanReadableCopyright is the one Finder's Get Info panel shows, and the
# generated plist has no such key.
Info.plist.NSHumanReadableCopyright  $Copyright
Info.plist.NSPrincipalClass          NSApplication
Info.plist.LSMinimumSystemVersion    11.0
Info.plist.LSApplicationCategoryType public.app-category.utilities
Info.plist.NSHighResolutionCapable   true

PostBuild
{
    Copy assets/* Build/Sundial.app/Contents/Resources
}
